Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public.

Bonus Lesson #1: Credit Education

Hey there Bruce! You've made it through the first 9 lessons of
Dispute 21!!! At this point, you should be an FDCPA Master. We've
covered a variety of topics related to the Fair Debt Collection
Practices Act including: 

**understanding the process of Validation**, 

**what is considered proper validation**, 

**court cases and verdicts based on the FDCPA that we can reference in
situations when the law is not "clear"** , 

**the difference between regular collection accounts and credit card
collections and how to handle them, how debt collectors are governed by
the FDCPA, the tricky practices they use to collect and how their
actions violate you rights and your client's rights under the FDCPA**,
and finally 

**the best industry practices in dealing with debt collectors to stop
them from contacting your clients and to cease collection on debts**. 

We started with the FDCPA first because a collection account is the most
common negative account type on the credit report. Whether you're
preparing to take on your first client or you already have clientele and
are just looking for the best way to serve them, understanding the ins
and outs of collection accounts, how to dispute them, how to handle debt
collectors and how to educate your clients on dealing with debt
collectors will give you the biggest bang for your buck!

From here we'll move into the Fair Credit Reporting Act and then on to
the Dispute Resolution Process and Dispute Flows. But first, we're
going to get into some credit education. I've mentioned before that
although this course was originally designed to be 21 days, you would
get some additional bonus content that will be useful to the overall
functioning of your business as well as to increase your knowledge as a
credit professional. Some of your bonus content will contain credit
education, facts, and explanations to help you understand the foundation
on which this industry was built and to help you in your quest of
becoming a Credit Expert. Other bonus content will contain information
regarding the various credit reporting agencies and what types of
information you may find on their credit reports to assist you in the
dispute process, advanced dispute tactics, and dispute reasons and
letter language to insert into the dispute flows we will discuss in
later lessons to assist you in your processing of client files. 

Being that we are moving into studying the FCRA and the Dispute Process,
in today's bonus content we'll take a look at what goes on behind
the scenes of the dispute process and how the Credit Bureaus created a
system to trick consumers into voluntarily waiving their rights of the
dispute process, knowledge I gained when studying and learning from
industry vet, Mike Citron. By simply explaining what you're about to
learn to a room of consumers when I speak at home buyers seminars,
workshops, conferences, etc., I'm able to win over the trust of
consumers and turn them into clients. Pair this with all of the other
credit information you will learn throughout this course and you will
definitely be equipped to speak as the Expert you are. As you read
today's lesson, consider how you can utilize this information to help
you grow your business! 

Behind the Scenes: The Dispute Process

How Automated Disputes Hurt the Consumer and Why We're Advised to
NEVER Dispute Online

When learning to dispute items on the credit report, one of the first
rules a new repair specialist is likely to hear is "Never Dispute
Online!" While this advice is rampant throughout the community, many
still do not understand the "why" behind it. 

One of the many ways the credit bureaus have found to defraud the public
is by introducing an online system that was supposedly created to "Help"
consumers. This automated dispute system requires that the consumer log
onto the dispute site and enter in the information regarding the
erroneous items. The consumer dispute is then converted to a two-three
digit code. The code is then forwarded to the reporting agency for
investigation. 

In reality, what it does is strip the consumer's legal rights, while
making it easy for the credit bureaus to further their agenda. At first
glance, the online disputing system appears to be a benefit to the
consumer. The only benefit to the consumer is the ease and convenience
of filing an online dispute instead of actually writing a letter to
dispute the item. That part is a benefit, but disputing your items
online is actually a great disadvantage to the consumer due to the part
of the process that appears on the surface to be a benefit. 

The 30-day time limit-
The FCRA imposes a 30-day time limit on the dispute. If the item is
not responded to within that time limit, it must be removed from your
credit file. Using the automated dispute system makes it very easy for
the credit-reporting bureau and reporting agency to not only respond,
but to respond without having to do a full investigation...

Paper  Trail-
Using  the  automated  dispute  system  does  not  create  a  paper  trail  for  the
consumer to track the details of the dispute. As a result, the consumer
does not have a paper trail if they have to take further legal actions
for non-compliance by the credit bureaus.

Limited Dispute Reasons-
Due to the electronic coding system, there is an inherent limitation for
disputing. The coding system is limited to the codes that are in
existence. There is no place for you to send all relevant information
about the dispute to the furnisher of data. Under the FCRA, all relevant
information must be transmitted to the data furnisher. The online
dispute system circumvents that requirement.  

Expeditious Dispute Resolution-
The Fair Credit Reporting Act section 611a(8) changes the standard
requirements and protections afforded to the consumer by the FCRA when
the consumer uses the online dispute process. 

In the FCRA the "Expeditious Dispute Resolution" section states

"The agency shall not be required to comply with paragraphs 2, 6, and 7
with respect to that dispute if they delete the trade line within 3
days."

Paragraph  2  requires  the  credit reporting agency to forward
your dispute and all related documentation you provide to the creditor.
The online dispute process does not allow for any paperwork or
documentation to be provided nor considered in the process. 

What  consumers  are  not  aware  of  is  credit-reporting  agencies
will sometimes delete an item without forwarding your dispute to the
data furnisher, the data furnisher will then re-report this item in the
next reporting cycle. The data furnisher will most likely not be aware
of your dispute therefore they may simply re-insert the item. There is a
standard notice that is required to be sent prior to any reinsertion of
a deleted item, however based on the law, if you dispute online, the
credit reporting agencies DO NOT have to follow that part of the law.
The Credit reporting agencies DO NOT have to send you a notice prior to
reinsertion if you dispute online. This information that would protect
consumers from their federally granted rights being stripped from them
was barely accessible UNTIL NOW! Bruce, now that you know and
understand, it is your responsibility to educate uninformed consumers. 

Paragraph 6 requires the credit-reporting agency to provide you with
written results. When you dispute online, the reporting agency is not
required to comply with this.

Paragraph  7  requires  the credit reporting agency to provide you
with the method of verification on request from the consumer. As you
will learn during an upcoming lesson on "Method of Verification," this
tactic is a powerful tool in the dispute process. However, if
"Expeditious Dispute Resolution" is used, the right to utilize this
tactic is eliminated.

Bruce, can you see how acquiring and using this knowledge can make
you the credit expert? Can you see how you can use this information to
win over the trust of clients? Most people just tell you that you
shouldn't dispute online, but rarely can they explain why and how
doing so could harm the client. Well, now you can explain as well as
back it up using the LAW! 

Bruce, I'd love to hear the ways in which you've been able to
benefit from this course content so far and/or how you feel you can use
this content moving forward. Feel free to reach out to me if you have
any questions or to simply share your thoughts, Ah Ha Moments, and
Successes at elle@educreditsolutions.com
